I'll review EVAL-06. Let me first get the full context from Redline.

I can see the design describes an ETL pipeline using a database service account with full admin rights across all production databases. This is a significant security concern - excessive privileges create a large blast radius if compromised.

Let me build the threat model:

```json
{
  "schema": "redline.threatmodel.v1",
  "issueKey": "EVAL-06",
  "sketch": {
    "components": ["ETL Pipeline", "Service Account", "Production Databases", "Credential Store"],
    "dataStores": ["Production Databases"],
    "externalServices": [],
    "trustBoundaries": ["ETL Pipeline to Production Databases"],
    "dataClasses": [],
    "exposure": "internal",
    "llmFeatures": false
  },
  "threats": [
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Service account has excessive admin privileges across all production databases",
      "scenario": "The ETL service account is configured with full admin rights on every production database. If the ETL process is compromised through a code injection vulnerability, software supply chain attack, or credential theft, an attacker gains admin control over all production data. They can read, modify, or delete any data, create backdoor accounts, or exfiltrate sensitive information across the entire data estate.",
      "component": "Service Account",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The ETL pipeline runs with a database service account that has full admin rights on every production database"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.1.3", "V4.3.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-250", "CWE-269"],
      "plainLanguage": "The ETL account can do anything to any production database. If someone hacks the ETL system or steals its password, they control all your production data.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Limit service account to minimum required privileges",
        "given": "the ETL pipeline needs to create tables and write data",
        "when": "configuring the database service account",
        "then": "grant only CREATE TABLE and INSERT/UPDATE permissions on specific schemas, not full admin rights"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Stolen service account credentials expose all production data",
      "scenario": "An attacker obtains the service account credentials through phishing a developer, exploiting a vulnerability in the credential store, or finding them in logs or configuration files. With admin rights on all databases, they can query and exfiltrate all production data across every database without triggering database-level access controls or permissions that would apply to individual users.",
      "component": "Service Account",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The ETL pipeline runs with a database service account that has full admin rights on every production database"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.10.1", "V6.4.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-522", "CWE-311"],
      "plainLanguage": "If someone gets the ETL password, they can read everything in every production database without anyone noticing it's not the real ETL.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Implement credential rotation and secure storage",
        "given": "the service account must authenticate to production databases",
        "when": "storing and using credentials",
        "then": "store credentials in a secrets vault (AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault), rotate them every 90 days, and use short-lived tokens where possible"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Tampering",
      "title": "Compromised ETL process can modify or destroy all production data",
      "scenario": "An attacker exploits a vulnerability in the ETL code (such as SQL injection, deserialization flaw, or malicious dependency) to execute arbitrary database commands. With full admin rights, they can DROP tables, UPDATE critical records to corrupt data integrity, or ALTER schemas to introduce backdoors. The design explicitly mentions avoiding permission changes for convenience, meaning there are no constraints to limit damage.",
      "component": "ETL Pipeline",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "database service account that has full admin rights on every production database, so new tables work without permission changes"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V5.3.4", "V5.3.8"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-89", "CWE-915"],
      "plainLanguage": "If a bug or attack lets someone run code in the ETL, they can delete or change any data in production because the account has no limits.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Segregate privileges and implement change controls",
        "given": "the ETL must modify production data",
        "when": "the pipeline runs",
        "then": "use separate accounts for read-only operations vs schema changes, require approval workflows for DDL statements, and log all admin-level operations"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Repudiation",
      "title": "Cannot attribute malicious actions to specific users when using shared service account",
      "scenario": "Multiple developers or systems share access to the service account credentials for debugging or manual ETL runs. When suspicious database modifications occur (data deletion, unauthorized schema changes), it's impossible to determine who actually performed the action because all activity appears to come from the same service account. An insider threat or compromised developer account cannot be traced.",
      "component": "Service Account",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The ETL pipeline runs with a database service account"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V7.1.1", "V7.1.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-778"],
      "plainLanguage": "If something bad happens to the database, you can't tell who did it because everyone using the ETL looks the same in the logs.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Implement individual accountability for database access",
        "given": "humans need to debug or run ETL processes",
        "when": "accessing production databases",
        "then": "require developers to use their own credentials with appropriate permissions, never share service account credentials, and log the original user identity for all operations"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "DenialOfService",
      "title": "Admin privileges allow complete destruction of production data availability",
      "scenario": "An attacker who compromises the ETL pipeline or steals its credentials can execute DROP DATABASE or TRUNCATE TABLE commands across all production systems. Because the account has admin rights everywhere and the design prioritizes convenience over control, there are no safeguards to prevent catastrophic data loss. Recovery depends entirely on backup systems, and the attack could target backups if the same account has access.",
      "component": "Production Databases",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "full admin rights on every production database, so new tables work without permission changes"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.1.3"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-250"],
      "plainLanguage": "An attacker who breaks into the ETL can delete entire production databases, taking down all services that depend on them.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Restrict destructive operations and implement safeguards",
        "given": "the service account should not need to drop databases or truncate tables in normal operation",
        "when": "configuring account permissions",
        "then": "deny DROP and TRUNCATE permissions, require manual approval for such operations, and implement point-in-time recovery with write-once backups"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Single point of failure credential enables lateral movement across all databases",
      "scenario": "An attacker gains initial access to a low-privilege part of the system (web application vulnerability, compromised developer workstation) and discovers the service account credentials in environment variables, config files, or memory dumps. These credentials immediately provide admin access to all production databases, enabling lateral movement across the entire data infrastructure without needing to compromise each database individually.",
      "component": "Credential Store",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "database service account that has full admin rights on every production database"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.10.3", "V6.4.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-522", "CWE-266"],
      "plainLanguage": "If the ETL password leaks anywhere in your systems, attackers can jump straight to controlling all production databases without breaking into each one separately.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Implement defense-in-depth with separate credentials per database",
        "given": "the ETL accesses multiple production databases",
        "when": "designing the authentication architecture",
        "then": "use separate service accounts per database or database cluster with minimum required permissions, so compromising one account doesn't grant access to all systems"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

This threat model identifies six critical security issues with the design, all stemming from the excessive privilege anti-pattern. The most severe threats are the elevation of privilege and information disclosure risks (likelihood 4, impact 5), as a single compromised credential provides admin access to all production data.

